Where the Credit Is: Why Running Counts Teach What Final Counts Miss Anonymous Author(s) Affiliation Address email Abstract A Transformer can represent a counting algorithm and still struggle to learn it from final answers. On counting modulo a small number, showing the running counts makes the task much easier to learn. We explain this gap through the information each training signal carries. Every letter hidden from a feature contracts its correlation with the final count, while near initialization the Transformer’s output is dominated by features that read only a few letters. A displayed count exposes the next update directly. In a two-block Transformer that holds exact solutions for both formats, outcome gradient flow from a zero readout stays near chance for a time exponential in the number of letters. With a gate that reads the displayed count and the current letter, simultaneous gradient flow from small random magnitudes along structured directions learns the supervised transitions after polynomial time. This holds for every modulus when match and non-match probabilities differ; the proof supplies the routing and gate-readout direction. Experiments test what happens beyond these restrictions: step supervision helps on counting, other state-tracking tasks, and LoRA-tuned Qwen2.5-0.5B on BIG-Bench Hard object tracking. It does not help at every size, and one reversal remains unexplained. 1 Introduction A model that can count need not learn to count from final answers. In our experiments, the same Transformer succeeds when shown running counts and stays near chance when trained on the final count alone. Yet we can write down exact parameters for either computation. If both solutions are available, what does showing the steps give the learner? Intermediate supervision often makes reasoning tasks easier to learn [Nye et al., 2021, Wei et al., 2022, Lightman et al., 2023]. The usual intuition is that steps break a long computation into smaller ones. We ask what this means for the gradient of a fixed network. Counting a query letter modulo K lets us examine that question closely: the answer-only and step-by-step tasks share the same counting process, architecture, initialization and training budget (Figure 2b, Figure 1a). The key is what a feature learns from the letters it reads. Averaging over every other letter contracts its correlation with the final count by a factor λ < 1 per unseen letter. Near initialization, the output is dominated by terms that each read one letter; combining more letters costs powers of the initialization scale (Figure 1b). Final answers thus supply very little credit to the features the network can readily change. A displayed count and the current letter, however, expose the next update. The two paths are outcome: step: hidden letters → few-letter features decorrelate → tiny credit → plateau, displayed count → local innovation → accessible credit → learned transition → solution. Submitted to 40th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2026). Do not distribute. (b) Degree mismatch Exact executor Step Energy share Exact accuracy (%) (a) Training 8/10 seeds solve Outcome Chance Target Ar 10−3 Jacobian Jr 10−6 Degree r Updates (thousands) Figure 1: Same architecture and task, different training behavior. (a) Counting with n = 16, K = 2 and zero readout: median exact continuation accuracy and interquartile band over ten paired seeds, 8k updates; the dashed line marks the exact executor that both formats admit (Proposition 1). (b) A separate n = 8, K = 2, ε = 0.02 measurement with nonzero readout: target and Jacobian energy occupy different ANOVA degrees, the number of letters a component depends on jointly (Appendix E.3). Contributions. • Unseen letters erase outcome credit (Section 3). A feature that reads a set S of letters has correlation at most λn−|S| with the final count, so near initialization outcome credit decays like λn ; the upper bound and leading coefficient of step credit for the counting rule carry no such factor (Theorems 1 and 2). • Outcome training plateaus (Theorem 3). From a zero readout, gradient flow stays near chance under any block schedule, the relative bias included, for time Ω(λ−n ), in a Transformer wide enough to hold the outcome executor. • Step training converges (Theorem 4). For a gate head that reads the displayed count and the current letter, joint gradient flow on its query, key and both readouts, from small random magnitudes along hand-coded directions, reaches positive margins on every count in a time polynomial in the initialization scale and the head dimension, with no other dependence on n. • Experiments (Section 5) test each prediction: the exponential plateau, outcome accuracy at the low-degree prior, learnable routing, and step supervision beyond counting, including a pretrained model. Section 6 places this fixed-target comparison alongside work on parity and intermediate supervision. 2 Setup Modular counting keeps the state space fixed as the prompt grows. What increases is the number of updates hidden behind the answer. We can therefore lengthen the computation without changing the prediction problem at its end. Definition 1 (Counting modulo K). A prompt has a start count s0 , uniform on ZK , and letters w1 , . . . , wn drawn i.i.d. from an alphabet in which a fixed query letter w⋆ has probability p ∈ (0, 1). With bt = 1[wt = w⋆ ], st = st−1 + bt (mod K), t = 1, . . . , n, and the answer is y = sn . Definition 2 (Contraction coefficient). For counting modulo K, λ = max 1 − p + p e2πij/K = 1 − p + p e2πi/K . 1≤j<K (1) (2) Averaging over one unseen letter contracts every nonconstant Fourier mode of the count by at most λ (Figure 2); in information-theoretic terms, λ is the maximal correlation of consecutive counts and λ2 1−p (a) (b) Outcome: supervise sn only p 1−p p K−1 s0 1−p s1 w2 s2 ··· wn sn n hidden letters: each Fourier mode contracts by ≤ λ per letter Step: supervise every st from the displayed st−1 p p w1 s0 w1 s1 w2 s2 ··· wn sn 1−p st = st−1 + bt (mod K) T = (1 − p) I + p R one local transition (st−1 , wt ) 7→ st : innovation ≥ 1 − λ2 Figure 2: One counting process, two views of it. (a) Counting modulo K is a cyclic Markov chain: each letter keeps the count with probability 1 − p and advances it with probability p, so the transition matrix is T = (1 − p)I + pR with R the cyclic shift. Its nonconstant Fourier modes contract by at most λ per step (Appendix C.2). (b) Outcome supervision labels only sn , so the label depends on the letters through n unseen steps of the chain and its correlation with any feature of k letters shrinks like λn−k (Theorem 1). Step supervision displays st−1 and labels st , so each label is one step of the chain and keeps an innovation of at least 1 − λ2 (Theorem 2). the χ2 contraction coefficient of one unseen letter [Witsenhausen, 1975, Raginsky, 2016]. The complementary quantity 1 − λ2 is the variance a displayed count leaves to the next letter (Equation (4)). For four uniform letters, λ = 0.5 at K = 2 and 0.66 at K = 3. Formats. Both formats see the same prompt. The outcome loss Lout is the cross-entropy of the final count; the step loss Lstep is the teacher-forced cross-entropy of the transitions st−1 , wt 7→ st for t ≥ 2; the first prediction is a boundary case whose previous count sits in the prompt (Definition A.1). Experiments supervise every running count unless stated otherwise. Model. A causal Transformer with two blocks of softmax heads and ReLU MLPs, a relative position bias initialized at zero, no LayerNorm, arbitrary widths and a linear readout (Assumption 1), initialized at θ0 = εθ̃ with fan-in operator-norm bounds and MLP biases of order one (Assumption 2). The biases fix every ReLU’s sign on a ball Bε of radius √ proportional to ε, provided ε ≤ ε0 = c min(1, N1b ) with Nb the MLP bias norm (of order n at executor width, Appendix C.6), so near initialization the MLPs act linearly; the AdamW experiments drop this condition (Appendix C.1). Constants c, C depend on neither the widths, n nor ε. Proposition 1 (Both solutions exist). For every K, alphabet size and n, one architecture as in ⋆ ⋆ Assumption 1, with widths linear in n, has parameters θstep and θout at which greedy decoding produces the correct step and outcome continuations on every prompt, with a positive logit margin. 2.1 The exact executors make the training failure more puzzling. One program updates the state locally; the other aggregates the match bits before applying modular arithmetic. Both fit in this Transformer. We still have to explain why training reaches one so much more readily than the other. Two exact programs in one Transformer Executor Step Outcome Block 1 ⋆ bt = 1[wt = w ] every bt Block 2 Output (lookup, modulo K) route st−1 and bt average b1 , . . . , bn ; route s0 st = st−1 + Pbt s n = s 0 + t bt Two primitives implement them: positional attention with scores of order log n on a chosen source set copies one register or averages several, and one ReLU unit per count–bit pair performs the modular lookup. Routing leakage is O(n−2 ); Appendix B gives the literal tensor entries and the error calculation, certifying a logit margin greater than 19 on every prompt. 86 3 Credit through unseen letters To understand the training gap, we first ask how much information about the target reaches a parameter update. Fix the start count, so the letters are independent. Definition 3 (Credit). For a target ξ, a random vector in RK , let Φξ (θ) = E[hzθ , ξi]. The  ∂z signal  credit for ξ is k∇θ Φξ k = E ( ∂θθ )⊤ ξ . For the outcome signal ξ = y − u (one-hot answer minus uniform), credit is the answer-dependent part of the gradient of Lout (Lemma C.1; Shalev-Shwartz et al., 2017, Abbe et al., 2025). 3.1 What a feature misses. Suppose a feature H reads only the letters in a set S. Even if it uses those letters perfectly, it must average over every letter outside the set. By the tower property, E[H⊤ (y − u)] = E[H⊤ E[y − u | wS ]], and given wS the n − |S| unseen letters still move the count: each multiplies every nonconstant Fourier mode of its distribution by at most λ (Figure 2a). Hence   (3) E H⊤ (y − u) ≤ λn−|S| kHkL2 (Lemma C.2). Each omitted letter has the same multiplicative cost; seeing a few letters cannot bypass the rest of the computation. The exponent is sharp. Outcome: few-letter features cannot see the final count The network reads few letters at a time. Scale every attention score by ζ ∈ [0, 1] and expand in ζ. At ζ = 0 attention is uniform and the MLPs are affine, so the network is additive over positions: each term reads at most one letter. Every interaction among letters passes through the scores, and a term with k ≥ 1 scores reads at most 2k + 1 letters and costs ε2+4k in the Jacobian (Proposition C.1). Applying Equation (3) term by term bounds the outcome credit. Call the credit of the score-dependent part Jscore of the Jacobian the score-mediated credit: it contains every term that reads two or more letters, and also score-dependent terms that read one. Theorem 1 (Unseen letters erase outcome credit). Under Assumptions 1 to 3, with the start count fixed: (i) for every set S of letters and every square-integrable H that depends only on the letters in S, Equation (3) holds; (ii) for all n ≥ 2, ε2 ≤ cλ and θ ∈ Bε , the score-mediated credit is at most Cε6 λn−3 , and if ε ≤ cλ the whole outcome credit is at most Cελn . 3.2 A large step gradient could be misleading. The displayed count alone predicts the two possible next states and their probabilities. Fitting that prior lowers the loss without learning how to use the current letter. We separate that easy signal from the rule itself. With the state-only predictor qs = (1 − p)es + p es+1 , write est − u = (qst−1 − u) + gt , where the rule signal  gt = est − qst−1 = (bt − p) est−1 +1 − est−1 Step: a local innovation survives has covariance, on the centred state subspace 1⊥ := {v ∈ RK : 1⊤ v = 0}, Cov(gt )  1 − λ2 I K on 1⊥ . (4) Thus an outcome target pays λn−|S| for the letters a feature does not read, whereas a displayed transition pays the local innovation 1 − λ2 only once. Let Φrule be the credit functional with the rule signal as target. Theorem 2 (Step credit). Under Assumptions 1 to 3 with a uniform start, for all n ≥ 2 and θ ∈ Bε : (i) (prior-fitting gradient) if ε ≤ cλ, then k∇θ Lstep (θ)k ≥ c ελ while k∇θ Lout (θ)k ≤ Cε(ε2 + λn ); 6 (ii) (rule credit) if ε ≤ c and attention uses the full causal prefix, then k∇θ Φrule (θ)k ≤ Cε n ; if moreover p < 12 , the degree-6 polynomial Pn is not identically zero, with an explicit count×letter monomial of coefficient of order n√1d (Lemma C.8). h Part (i) fits the count-only prior. Part (ii) isolates the rule signal: its upper bound is of order εn , without the factor λn , and its nonzero leading coefficient shows that the signal can survive. Its size at random initialization remains a separate question. The bound becomes two-sided at any direction 1/4 ε6 γrule with |Pn (θ̃)| ≥ γrule for ε ≤ c min(γrule ε0 , γrule ) (Remark C.2); n , where k∇θ Φrule (εθ̃)k ≥ 2n we do not prove such a margin at random initialization. Measurements are in Appendix E.2. 4 Which solution training reaches Small initial credit alone does not imply slow learning: the parameters might reach a region with a stronger signal. We ask whether outcome flow can get there quickly, and whether step flow can turn its local signal into a learned transition. 4.1 The next theorem controls the trajectory as it tries to leave initialization. A local loss floor limits how far gradient flow can travel. Theorem 3 (Exponential-time outcome plateau near small initialization). Under Assumptions 1 to 3 with a zero initial readout, let P (t) be, at each time, the orthogonal projection onto a set of trainable parameter blocks, piecewise constant in t; the blocks may include the relative bias. Consider gradient flow dθ dt = −P(t)∇θ Lout (θ); full joint flow (P ≡ I) and training only the blocks of Theorem 4 are special cases. For all n ≥ 2, ε ≤ cλ and 0 ≤ t ≤ cλ−n , with constants that do not depend on the schedule: Outcome: an exponential plateau (i) (locality) kθ(t) − θ0 k ≤ r0 ε, the relative bias included in the norm; (ii) (loss) Lout (θ(t)) ≥ log K − Cε2 λn ;   + Cελn/2 . (iii) (correct-answer probability) E softmax(zθ(t) )y ≤ K The theorem bounds the length of the plateau; it does not assert that outcome training never converges. Proof idea. Along the flow, for any schedule,  dLout = −kP(t)∇θ Lout k2 , so kθ(t) − θ0 k2 ≤ t Lout (θ0 ) − Lout (θ(t)) . dt This gives the weak signal a consequence beyond the first update. Near θ0 , the outcome loss can fall by at most Cε2 λn (Lemma C.3), even with a trained relative bias (Lemma C.4). That small decrease limits the distance the flow can travel: leaving the ball takes time of order λ−n (Appendix C.4). 4.2 For step training, the question is whether the surviving signal is enough to learn the update. We supply the routing to isolate this question. One block-2 head, the gate head, has a fixed mask: the position holding st−1 and predicting st attends only to itself and the current letter. Its query, key and value start along hand-coded directions that read the count, read the  letter and carry the count, respectively (Appendix D). With xt = est−1 and rb = σ −(−1)b a , the state logits are exactly (Lemma D.1) ε2 qk zt = εUxt + εv rbt Wxt , a= √ , dh where U and W = w(I − R) are K × K readout blocks for the count and for the gate output, and q, k, v are the scalar query, key and value factors of the gate. The weight rbt that the gate keeps on the displayed count depends on the current letter through a, so the gate output is a count×letter product of strength a. Step: joint gradient flow learns the transition 167 Initialization and training. The seed fixes directions, not magnitudes: 12 ε ≤ |q(0)|, |k(0)|, |v| ≤ 2ε with arbitrary signs, and both readouts start at zero, so the initial prediction is uniform and |a(0)| is of order ε4 . Having the right directions does not guarantee a useful initial interaction: the query and key can have unfavorable signs, and the zero readouts initially carry no prediction. One Euclidean gradient flow trains (U, W, q, k) simultaneously, with no stages and no stopping rule; the gate readout is trained along its transition direction I − R, like the query, key and value (for K = 2 this is no restriction), and v and all other parameters stay at the seed. Theorem 4 (Joint gradient flow learns the transition). Count modulo K ≥ 2 with n ≥ 2 letters and a query of probability p ∈ (0, 1), p 6= 21 , and let h⋆ = | log 1−p p |, pmin = min(p, 1 − p) and gap ∈ (0, 1]. Call the initialization aligned if q(0)k(0)(1 − 2p) < 0 and misaligned otherwise, and let √ gap pmin h⋆ ε⋆ = (aligned), ε⋆ = (misaligned). 712 (1 + log K) For 0 < ε ≤ ε⋆ , and in the misaligned case |D| ≥ gap ε2 with D = q(0)2 − k(0)2 , joint gradient flow on Lstep gives every prediction positive margin against all K − 1 other counts at every time t ≥ T , and Lstep → 0, where, with an absolute constant C, γ = gap (misaligned) or γ = 1 (aligned), √ dh log dεh C K log K  T ≤ 4 + . ε pmin min{1, h⋆ } γ p2min T depends on n only through dh (any dh ≥ 1 is allowed). If |q(0)| and |k(0)| are independent and ε ε uniform on [ 2 , 2], with any signs, then Pr(|D| < gap ε ) ≤ 3 gap, so for ε ≤ ε⋆ (misaligned value) the conclusion holds with probability at least 1 − 43 gap; the randomness is over the scalar magnitudes and signs, not the directions. Why joint training succeeds. Cyclic symmetry reduces the simultaneous flow to four scalars: the count-readout contrast c, the background level s of the count readout (absent for K = 2), the gate-readout contrast g, and the query–key strength a (Lemma D.3). The gate gradient splits as ∂Lstep 1 ∂Lstep a = + tanh( ) err, ∂g 2 ∂c ∂Lstep = g σ ′ (a) err, ∂a where err > 0 is the probability-weighted prediction error, including the background counts. The count-only prior first gives g a direction to move; the local innovation supplies the letter-dependent term. The second identity makes the query–key strength move against the sign of g. A misaligned gate can therefore cross into a useful regime while the count readout fits the prior (Lemma D.9). But crossing once is not enough: the gate readout must survive as the other parameters keep moving. A monotone balance between g and c keeps g ≥ 0 once a < 0 (Lemma D.8) and eventually holds g above a fixed floor (Lemma D.10). That floor lets |a| grow exponentially to order one. The gradient lower bound proportional to tanh2 ( a2 ) then gives positive margins after time O(ε−4 log 1ε ), consistent with the measured ε−4.0 to ε−4.3 (Lemma D.11 and Table 5). These are phases of the same simultaneous flow, rather than training stages chosen by the analyst; an aligned start bypasses the crossing phase, starting aligned at t = 0. Matched separation. Take the same architecture, mask, seed and trained set (U, W, q, k), and ε ≤ min(ε⋆ , cλ). By Corollary D.1 and Theorem 4, Tout ≥ c λ−n (any block schedule, relative bias included), √ dh log dεh K log K  C + , Tstep ≤ 4 ε pmin min{1, h⋆ }γ p2min where Tout is the time outcome flow stays near chance and Tstep the time after which step flow has positive margins. At executor widths dh = Θ(n), Theorem 3 needs ε ≤ c min(λ, n−1/2 ) (Appendix C.6); with ε at that value, Tstep is polynomial in n, of order n5/2 log n for fixed λ, p, K and γ, not independent of n, while Tout is exponential. The mask gives the gate head its routing; whether joint training also learns which letter to read is open, and the experiments below test it at finite step size. λ−n Step Horizon n Spearman ρ = 0.91 Above prior (points) Outcome Outcome accuracy (%) Displacement time τ (0.1) (c) Above the prior (b) Answer bias (a) Plateau duration State-only prior Chance 10−5 10−3 10−1 Remaining bias λn Spearman ρ = 0.15 State-only prior −20 10−5 10−3 10−1 Remaining bias λn Figure 3: Outcome escape is slow; outcome training learns the low-degree prior and little else. (a) Zero-readout counting, K = 2, λ = 0.5, ε = 0.002: median time to a displacement of 0.1εkθ̃k and interquartile band over three draws; the dashed λ−n guide is anchored at n = 4. The step exit reflects fitting the count-only prior, not learning the rule. (b) Outcome exact accuracy after 8k AdamW updates in 20 horizon–alphabet cells: means and seed ranges over three seeds; the green n curve is the state-only prior 1+λ 2 , and gray is chance. (c) The same accuracies minus the prior. Correlations use cell means. 5 Experiments The experiments ask where the explanation survives a change of optimizer, initialization or task, and where it stops being enough. They use finite-step training; the AdamW runs have learned absolute positions without the relative bias. Protocols are in Appendix E. Does outcome escape exponentially slowly? The plateau proof concerns continuous time and a small ball. We first check whether its escape mechanism is visible with finite updates. In a compact model built to approximate the theorems’ assumptions, with full-batch gradient descent in float64 and no ReLU sign change on any input along the trajectory, the outcome exit time grows at least as fast as λ−n , both from the ball Bε of Theorem 3 and at a displacement of 0.1εkθ̃k, while the step exit time stays constant (Figure 3a, Table 7), because the step loss can leave the ball by fitting the count-only prior; halving the update length changes the exit time by less than 1%. At initialization, 99.6% of the Jacobian’s energy is at degree 0 (Figure 1b), and the answer-dependent gradient decays by a fitted 0.48–0.51 per letter at λ = 0.5 over three seeds (Table 9). Under AdamW, with a zero readout and paired seeds, at n = 16, K = 2 and 8k updates, step training reaches 93.1% exact accuracy (8 of 10 seeds above 90%) and outcome training 49.8% (none). Across 120 counting runs at n = 6–16 with the default readout, outcome training solves none and step training 60 within 8k updates. Step training at ε = 0.02 is slow rather than stuck, as its ε6 rule credit suggests: every step run of Table 11(a) is solved by 16k updates, and at ε = 0.1, where ε6 is 1.6 × 104 times larger, every step seed solves within 8k. From initialization through 6k outcome updates, at most 2.4 × 10−6 of the Jacobian’s energy lies above degree 1 (Appendix E.3). Outcome failure is not universal at moderate λn : for counting modulo 3 at n = 8 (λn ≈ 0.036), outcome training solves two of three seeds within 32k updates. Does outcome learn only the accessible low-degree prior? Near-chance accuracy can hide some learning. To find out what outcome training has learned, we compare its accuracy with the prediction available from the start count alone. Varying the alphabet sets λ = 1 − A2 . Over 20 settings of n ∈ {6, . . . , 16} and λ ∈ {0.5, . . . , 0.9}, outcome accuracy has Spearman correlation 0.91 with λn n and −0.36 with n alone. Most of this correlation is the state-only prior 1+λ 2 , which predicting from the start count achieves: cell means lie between 2 points below and 7 points above it (median +0.2), and the excess has Spearman correlation only 0.15 with λn (Figure 3b,c). Once λn ≤ 0.04, every cell mean is within 1.5 points of the prior. Once λn is small, these runs show little improvement beyond that accessible prior. Together with the Jacobian measurements, this supports the picture of training that stays sensitive mainly to degree-0 and 1 features. Can ordinary finite-step training learn the routing? The positive theorem tells the gate where to look. Is that assistance necessary for learning, or mainly for our proof? Training the scores, the Table 1: Joint step training of the routing solves the task in most runs; outcome training under the same protocol solves none. Two-block Transformer of Assumption 1, counting modulo 2 with p = 14 ; only the scores, the relative bias and the readout are trained, by full-batch gradient descent (step size 2) from ε = 0.3, with the same seeds for both formats. A run is solved when every held-out prediction is correct. Step Outcome (300k updates) n Solved Updates to solve, median (range) Solved Answer accuracy (%) 5/5 5/5 4/5 2/2 44k (41–78k) 61k (46–99k) 77k (42–103k) 237k (208–266k) 0/5 0/5 0/5 0/2 50–94 50–55 50–51 relative bias and the readout jointly from a fan-in random initialization (ε = 0.3), every held-out count prediction becomes correct in 16 of 17 runs; the remaining run reaches 99.95% (Table 1); outcome training under the same protocol and seeds solves none of 17 runs within 300k updates and stays at chance for n ≥ 8. The median solve time grows from 44k updates at n = 6 to 237k at n = 16, where a factor λ−n would grow 1,024-fold. Does the same pattern appear beyond counting? Counting lets us prove the mechanism, but cannot tell us how far it travels. We test other tasks that hide a state behind a sequence of updates; these experiments probe the explanation beyond the theorem assumptions. Boolean-8 and a reversible register machine put 98.9% and 99.9% of their answer variance at degrees ≥ 2; outcome training fails on both while step training succeeds, and a first-occurrence control, which keeps a degree-1 part at every n, is learned from answers alone (Table 13; Figure 4 in Appendix E.5). With rank-8 LoRA on Qwen2.5-0.5B and the 750 real BIG-Bench Hard object-tracking items [Suzgun et al., 2022], outcome training stays at the majority-option rate at three objects (λ = 0, the answer is pure top degree) and reaches 15–24% at seven, while step training reaches 96–100% at both. At five objects the pattern breaks: outcome training reaches 80–94%, and one of three step runs fails (46%). Five objects have a smaller low-degree share (1.56%) than seven (3.34%), so λn does not order these sizes. We do not have an explanation for this reversal; it is a result the credit picture has yet to account for. 6 Related work Intermediate supervision. Scratchpads and process supervision [Nye et al., 2021, Uesato et al., 2022, Wei et al., 2022, Lightman et al., 2023] have been analysed through task decomposition [Wies et al., 2023], locality [Prystawski et al., 2023, Abbe et al., 2024] and expressivity [Feng et al., 2023, Merrill and Sabharwal, 2024, Malach, 2024]; outcome supervision need not be statistically harder [Jia et al., 2025], and chain supervision can improve sample complexity [Hanneke et al., 2026, Wen et al., 2025]; Wen et al. [2025] trace the gain to sparse dependence between tokens, which the running count makes local. We study how a fixed network distributes gradient credit. Gradient signal for hard targets. Shalev-Shwartz et al. [2017] show exponentially weak targetdependent gradients over families of composed targets. We ask how one fixed target supplies credit to a concrete Transformer, by tracking which letters each network term reads. Staircase and leap analyses [Abbe et al., 2021, 2022, 2023] characterize when SGD learns high-degree targets; for counting at λ > 0 every degree is present, and its size, not its absence, limits credit. Abbe et al. [2025] prove that noisy gradient descent on a two-layer network stays at chance on high-degree parities under Gaussian initialization, for the correlation loss; Theorem 3 concerns a Transformer under cross-entropy, with time λ−n from the contraction of the target. Panigrahi et al. [2025] show that intermediate teacher checkpoints supply low-degree signal for sparse parity; we analyse optimization rather than sample complexity. Li and Mossel [2025] use an Efron–Stein decomposition of the target; our proofs need no decomposition: they bound the overlap of the target with each network term directly, by conditioning on the letters the term reads, and we use ANOVA degree spectra only as a measurement (Appendix E.3). Closest in spirit, Hahn and Rofin [2024] show that Transformers are biased toward low-sensitivity functions, through a sharpness argument on the loss landscape; we 282 bound gradient credit through the letters each network term reads, and add a time bound. Spectral analyses of simplicity bias [Yang and Salman, 2019] find kernel eigenvalues on the Boolean cube that decay with degree, consistent with the low-degree Jacobian we measure. Chain of thought for parity and permutations. Kim and Suzuki [2025] prove that a one-layer Transformer with position-only attention and a fixed nonlinearity learns parity in one teacher-forced step, and that gradient methods fail without intermediate supervision for a parity drawn from a class. We ask why one format succeeds and the other fails for one fixed target in a model holding both solutions, with the computation learned. Wang et al. [2025] give a statistical-query lower bound and a curriculum result for compositions of hidden permutations; our displayed states permit constantdepth executors. Counting modulo K is a cyclic-group automaton, for which Liu et al. [2023] construct shallow shortcut Transformers; our outcome executor is such a shortcut, and the question is whether training finds it. 7 Discussion and conclusion 7.1 The exact executors give us a useful starting point: this Transformer can carry out either computation. The learning gap appears because the two formats give its parameters different information. Finalanswer credit contracts across the letters a feature does not read; from a zero readout, the resulting loss floor keeps outcome flow near chance for exponential time. A displayed count leaves a local update to learn. Its leading count–letter coefficient is nonzero for p < 12 under full-prefix attention, and the structured gate turns that signal into a transition learned in polynomial time. What the paper establishes Joint training of scores, relative bias and readout solves 16 of 17 step runs, and transfer experiments generally favor step supervision. These results push beyond the proof, but accessible credit, convergence with supplied routing, and discovery of that routing remain different claims. 7.2 The first open question is how the network learns where to look. A nonzero degree-6 coefficient does not show that simultaneous flow from random initialization amplifies it into routing. Table 1 gives finite-step evidence at ε = 0.3, but the positive theorem supplies the mask and structured directions. What remains open The other restrictions matter too. Theorems 1 and 2 concern small initialization with a fixed ReLU pattern; Theorem 3 adds full-batch gradient flow. The AdamW and routing experiments leave that regime. Theorem 4 covers every K ≥ 2 and transitions t ≥ 2, but trains the gate readout along I−R, together with the count readout and query and key; the value map and the rest of the network stay log 1 fixed. Its bound grows like ε−4 log 1ε , so the proved separation needs n of order log 1ε (Remark D.1). λ A full circulant gate readout for K ≥ 3 adds background terms of no fixed sign to the gate velocity (Appendix D.5). Every reduced flow we integrated still converges (Table 5); we do not yet know how to prove that broader result. There are empirical limits as well. Two of three outcome seeds solve counting modulo 3 at n = 8 within 32k updates. On BIG-Bench Hard, outcome training succeeds at five objects and one step run fails, reversing the pattern at three and seven even though the low-degree variance share is smaller at five. We leave that reversal unexplained. The controlled credit analysis gives us a way to ask what signal training receives, but does not yet predict the trajectories of pretrained models. Conclusion. Showing intermediate states changes which parts of a computation supply useful gradient credit. In counting, we can follow that change from a local signal to a separation in training time. The next question is how a network discovers the relevant computational structure when the routing is not supplied. Understanding that discovery would bring the mechanism closer to explaining when intermediate supervision helps reasoning beyond a controlled task. 327 References Emmanuel Abbe, Enric Boix-Adsera, Matthew Brennan, Guy Bresler, and Dheeraj Nagaraj. The staircase property: How hierarchical structure can guide deep learning. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS), 2021. Emmanuel Abbe, Enric Boix-Adsera, and Theodor Misiakiewicz. The merged-staircase property: A necessary and nearly sufficient condition for SGD learning of sparse functions on two-layer neural networks. In Conference on Learning Theory (COLT), pages 4782–4887, 2022. Emmanuel Abbe, Enric Boix-Adsera, and Theodor Misiakiewicz. SGD learning on neural networks: Leap complexity and saddle-to-saddle dynamics. In Conference on Learning Theory (COLT), volume 195 of Proceedings of Machine Learning Research, pages 2552–2623, 2023. Emmanuel Abbe, Samy Bengio, Aryo Lotfi, Colin Sandon, and Omid Saremi. How far can transformers reason? the globality barrier and inductive scratchpad, 2024. URL https://arxiv. org/abs/2406.06467. Emmanuel Abbe, Elisabetta Cornacchia, Jan Haz˛ła, and Donald Kougang-Yombi. Learning highdegree parities: The crucial role of the initialization. In International Conference on Learning Representations, 2025. Loubna Ben Allal, Anton Lozhkov, Elie Bakouch, Gabriel Martín Blázquez, Guilherme Penedo, Lewis Tunstall, Andrés Marafioti, Hynek Kydlíček, Agustín Piqueres Lajarín, Vaibhav Srivastav, et al. SmolLM2: When smol goes big – data-centric training of a small language model, 2025. Bradley Efron and Charles Stein. The jackknife estimate of variance. The Annals of Statistics, 9(3): 586–596, 1981. Guhao Feng, Bohang Zhang, Yuntian Gu, Haotian Ye, Di He, and Liwei Wang. Towards revealing the mystery behind chain of thought: A theoretical perspective. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS), 2023. Michael Hahn and Mark Rofin. Why are sensitive functions hard for transformers? In Proceedings