Proposition C.2(iii)), so every interaction among letters passes through the scores; the score-mediated credit is the credit of [0] θ of the Jacobian (Equation (9)), E[J⊤ the score-dependent part Jscore = ∂z score (y − u)] . ∂θ − J Architecture and initialization. Assumption 1 (Architecture). A causal Transformer with two blocks of softmax heads and ReLU MLPs, learned token and position embeddings, a learned relative position bias βi−j added to every attention score and initialized at zero, no LayerNorm, any widths (residual dres , head dh , MLP w) and a linear readout V; every logit vector is z = Vh with h independent of V. A head may have a fixed, input-independent causal submask; ordinary attention uses the full prefix. Assumption 2 (Initialization). Scale. θ0 = εθ̃ with ε ≤ ε0 := c min(1, N1b ), where every weight matrix and embedding block of θ̃ has operator norm at most a0 and the value-token embeddings have smallest singular value at least cemb > 0. Biases. Every MLP bias satisfies |b̃m | ≥ 1, and each MLP bias vector has norm at most Nb . Neighbourhood. We work on Bε = {θ : kθ − θ0 k ≤ r0 ε, β = 0} with r0 = min( 81 , cemb 4 ), where β is the relative position bias. Assumption 3 (Task). The task is counting modulo K ≥ 2 (Definition 1), so λ < 1. Constants and widths. On Bε , no ReLU unit changes sign (Appendix C.3). Constants c, C > 0 depend only on a0 , cemb , K and the numbers of blocks and heads, never on the widths, n, ε or the time. Fan-in-scaled initialization satisfies Assumption 2 w.h.p. √at every width, with a0 bounded in terms of the largest block aspect ratio, cemb ≥ 12 and Nb ≤ 2 w, provided the MLP biases are drawn with |b̃m | ∈ [1, 2], which default initializations (zero or small biases) do not do; at the executor width the aspect ratios are bounded, so the theorems hold there with n-independent constants for ε ≤ c min(λ, n−1/2 ) (Appendix C.6). Parameters and training. Assumption 4 (Training). Full-batch gradient flow dθ dt = −P∇θ L(θ) on Lout or Lstep , where P is the orthogonal projection onto the trainable parameter blocks, with the relative position bias held at its zero initialization. Theorem 3 drops this restriction and lets the bias train (Lemma C.4). Parameters carry the Euclidean norm of the vectorized parameters (Frobenius on each block). In Theorems 1 and 2 the relative bias is fixed at zero, which is what β = 0 in Bε and Assumption 4 θ encode, and θ denotes the parameters other than β: the Jacobian ∂z ∂θ , the gradients ∇θ L and ∇θ Φ, and the Jacobian terms of Proposition C.1 are taken with respect to these parameters only. The step-credit bounds use uniform zero-score attention through Proposition C.2(iv) and do not cover a trained bias. Theorem 3 allows the bias to be trained: its proof uses only the logit bounds, which hold for every β (Lemma C.4). The runs of Table 1 also train it; the gate head of Theorem 4 has no relative bias. The in-regime runs of Appendix E.2 have no relative bias. B Exact two-block executors This section proves Proposition 1. An implementation of the construction is in the accompanying code repository. 467 Architecture. Each block has three heads with dh = 2n + 6 (the number of positions) and one ReLU MLP of width max{|Σ|, 3K(n + 1), 2n + 1}; there is no LayerNorm: hi ← hi +  1  (h) (h) (h) softmaxj≤i √ (WQ hi )⊤ WK hj WV hj , d h h=1 j≤i 3 X X hi ← hi + Wout ReLU(Win hi + bin ), logitsi = V hi . Registers. The residual stream is the concatenation of one-hot registers tl |{z} tv pos qry match prev mt cnt s0 out , |{z} |{z} |{z} | {z } |{z} |{z} |{z} |{z} |{z} |Σ| K dh |Σ| K K dres = 2|Σ| + 4K + dh + 3. K r[a] is coordinate a of register r, and Ea←b copies b into a. Layout. Token ids: letters 0, . . . , |Σ| − 1, value v 7→ |Σ| + v, then SEP, “:”, EOS. Positions (0-indexed): prompt s0 @0, w⋆ @1, wt @(1 + t), SEP@(n + 2) step row st @(n + 2 + t), “:”@(2n + 3), sn @(2n + 4) outcome row “:”@(n + 3), sn @(n + 4) Position i predicts the token at i + 1: n + 1 + t predic