)P0 (bg)+pP1 (bg) , and the bracket is err: this is Lemma D.7(a). Off the line, the background terms carry g (0) + g (1) , which grows as the readout suppresses the K − 2 background counts, and need not have the sign of g. Table 5 integrates both versions. D.6 Controls and outcome training Letter direction. If ΠK = êh ê⊤ 0 instead, both letters have key εk êh and the gate weight does not depend on bt . As in Lemma D.3, both letter values then give ∆ = 0, and Lstep ≥ L0 (h) ≥ Hp for every ϑ. Corollary D.1 (Outcome training from the same initialization). Take the architecture and initialization of Theorem 4 for any K ≥ 2, with any fixed mask at the answer position, and λ as in Equation (2). Gradient flow on Lout , training (U, W, q, k) or any larger set of blocks (the relative bias included) under any block schedule, obeys the plateau bound of Theorem 3 for λ > 0 and ε in the range of that theorem. √ Proof. After division by ε, every map and embedding block has operator norm at most 2 K, the count embeddings have singular values 1, every MLP bias has |b̃m | = 1, and the readout is zero: this is Assumption 2. A fixed mask keeps every attention row a softmax over its visible sites (Assumption 1) and only lowers the numbers of scores and sites. Hence Proposition C.1, Proposition C.2(i)– (iii), Lemmas C.3 and C.5 and Theorem 3 apply unchanged for any schedule, and Lemma C.4 covers a trained bias. Remark D.1 (Scope). Trained and fixed. One flow trains U, W, q, k; the times ta , t3 are defined from the trajectory for the analysis and are not optimizer stages. The mask, which supplies the routing, and v are fixed. Corollary D.1 uses the same architecture, mask and seed, with the same trained set or a larger one. Dependence on n. The time bound and ε1⋆ are polynomial in 1ε , p(1−p) , h1⋆ , dh , gap and K, and depend on n only through dh ≥ 1. For the executor width dh = 2n + 6 they are polynomial in n, while outcome training stays near chance for time of order λ−n . The bound grows like ε−4 log 1ε , 985 and in Table 5 solve times grow like ε−4.0 to ε−4.3 , so the exponent is sharp up to the logarithm. log 1 The separation needs n of order log 1ε . λ Task. The theorem covers every K ≥ 2 and transitions t ≥ 2. At K = 2, λ = |1 − 2p| > 0 exactly when p 6= 21 . At p = 12 , h⋆ = 0: the count-only prior no longer separates the two candidate counts, the drift of h in Lemma D.7(e) vanishes, and nothing orients a misaligned gate; for K ≥ 3 this excludes p = 21 although λ > 0 there. The gate readout is trained along I − R (Appendix D.5); Table 5 shows the same behaviour for a full circulant gate readout. Whether joint training also learns the routing is open; Table 1 gives finite-step evidence at ε = 0.3. D.7 Numerical check Table 5 integrates Equation (18) for K = 2, 3, 4, 6, and for K = 3, 4, 6 also the flow with a full circulant gate readout, with dh = 8, v = ε and 1ε (|q(0)|, |k(0)|) ∈ {(1.3, 0.8) aligned, (1.3, 0.8) misaligned, (1, 0.98) misaligned}. A run is solved when every prediction has positive margin and Lstep ≤ L⋆ . The checked properties are those of the proof: g keeps its sign after prior fitting, a crosses zero at most once, and once the gate is aligned with |a| ≥ a8sc , ag ≤ 0 and |a| is nondecreasing. All 189 runs solve and pass. The aligned runs lie inside the range of Theorem 4; the misaligned ones, at ε ∈ {0.3, 0.2, 0.1}, lie outside it. The identities and constants of the proof are also checked mechanically (handcoded/theory_checks/theorem4_checks.py): ε⋆ implies Equation (19) and the scale relations of Appendix D.3 on a grid of (p, dh , gap, K) in 60-digit arithmetic; the derivative identities, the properties of ϕ and the gradient bound match finite differences; and the softmax model with full K × K count readout reproduces the reduced flow of Lemma D.3 for K = 2, 3, 4, 6. slope of log T in log 1ε (range) checks passed 3.1 · 106 3.9 · 106 4.7 · 106 5.7 · 106 4.04–4.33 4.07–4.27 4.07–4.24 4.06–4.19 27/27 27/27 27/27 27/27