 AdamW, lr 3 × 10−4 , batch 128 8,000 updates three or five rank 8 12,000 train default LoRA batch 16 800 updates 2001–2003 Table 6: Shared experimental settings. Task-specific exceptions are stated in the corresponding subsections. For counting, outcome runs supervise only the final count, while step runs supervise every running count, formatting token, final answer and EOS unless stated otherwise. The theory-only transition loss Lstep excludes the t = 1 boundary case; experimental step training supervises the full continuation. The joint routing runs in Appendix E.6 instead supervise every running count (Lall step ). Letter tasks use four letters unless stated otherwise; first-occurrence words contain the query. For GPT-2L and LoRA, seeds change only initialization, so their spread does not capture data variability. E.2 Purpose and setup. These runs approximate the regime of (A1)–(A4): the architecture, task and initialization conditions are enforced where they can be checked numerically, and gradient flow is approximated by refined full-batch updates. They test exponential outcome escape and step rule credit with polynomial horizon cost. The compact widths below test the gradient and exit mechanism only; exact executors are certified by Proposition 1 in the wider architecture, not at these widths. We use K = 2, two blocks of width 16, three heads of dimension 8, MLP width 16, |b̃m | ≥ 1, and two letters with match probability 41 (λ = 12 ). Gradients are exact over all words through n = 14, in full-batch float64, with step objective Lstep . Exit-time runs start from a zero readout; credit measurements retain a nonzero readout (Appendix C.5). Each update moves the parameters by 10−3 εkθ̃k. The exit time τ (r) is the accumulated step size when kθj − θ0 k = rεkθ̃k. We report it at r = 0.1 and at the radius r0 ε of the ball Bε in Theorem 3, r = r0 /kθ̃k ≈ 1.8 × 10−3 (r0 = 81 and kθ̃k ≈ 70 for all three draws); τ (0.1) is therefore a displacement time well outside Bε . Halving or quartering the update changes τ (0.1) by less than 1%. The exit from Bε is a much shorter distance, so it is integrated with updates of 10−6 εkθ̃k, about 1,800 updates to the boundary; updates of 10−5 change it by less than 0.5%. At every update before either exit, no ReLU pre-activation changes sign from its value at θ0 , on any input or position, so the fixed activation pattern of the analysis holds along the trajectory. Controlled test of the theorem regime Exit times. Figure 3a and Table 7 show outcome escape growing at least as fast as λ−n at both radii, while step escape remains nearly constant. Here ε = 0.002; the fast step exit reflects fitting the count-only prior. n=4 outcome, exit from Bε (r = r0 /∥θ̃∥) step, exit from Bε 2.8–6.8e2 1.4–3.4e3 outcome τ (0.1) step τ (0.1) 0.9–1.5e4 0.5–0.8e5 0.7–1.6e4 3.2–8.2e4 0.10–0.16 at every n 1.5–3.5e5 0.7–1.8e6 2.3–3.8e5 1.0–1.8e6 4.18–5.55 at every n 4.7–7.8e6 2.2–3.8e7 Table 7: Outcome escape grows exponentially while step escape stays nearly constant. ε = 0.002, zero readout; ranges are over three draws. Gradient decomposition. Rule credit has polynomial horizon cost; outcome interaction credit, measured as the credit at ANOVA degrees ≥ 2 (Appendix E.3), has exponential cost. Table 8 separates the O(ε) step prior, the O( εn ) useful rule credit, and the exponentially suppressed outcome interaction. These measurements use ε = 0.02 and are exact over all words. For a uniform start, the rule-to-outcome interaction credit ratios at n = 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14 are respectively 64–119, 129–268, 315–709, 803–2,000, 2,600–6,730, and 9,410–21,300. part of the gradient at θ0 Absorbed by single-position features step: prior outcome: total (degree 0 dominates) Requires interactions step: rule credit ∥∇θ Φrule ∥ outcome: degrees ≥ 2, start state fixed outcome: degrees ≥ 2, uniform start bound scale n=4 n=8 n = 12 ελ ελn 3.3e−2 1.6e−4 3.3e−2 6.6e−6 3.3e−2 3.2e−7 3.3e−9 1.4e−9 2.8e−11 1.8e−9 1.9e−10 2.6e−12 1.1e−9 1.6e−11 1.7e−13 ε6 n 6 n−3 ε λ ε6 λn−3 Table 8: Step rule credit is polynomial in horizon; outcome interaction credit is exponentially suppressed. Gradient norms at initialization for one draw of θ̃ (two letters, match probability 4 ), with their theorem scales (constants omitted; the scales are upper bounds). Over three draws, n∥∇θ Φrule ∥ ranges over 192–231, 141–195 and 97–169 for n = 4–14. The draws differ from those ε6 of Table 3. E.3 Degree as a measurement. The proofs do not use it, but the experiments report where the target and the Jacobian put their energy. With the start count fixed, every square-integrable P function f of the letters has a unique Hoeffding–Efron–Stein (ANOVA) decomposition f = S⊆[n] fS , P |S\U | with fS = (−1) E[f | w ] depending only on the letters in S and centred in U U ⊆S each of them [Hoeffding, 1948, Efron and Stein, 1981, O’Donnell, 2014]; its degree is |S|, and vectorand matrix-valued functions are decomposed entrywise. For the target, Ek(y − u)S k2 =  PK−1 Q 2(n−|S|) (1 − |ρj |2 )|S| : expand αjsn = αjs0 t ρj + (αjbt − ρj ) , whose term inj=1 |ρj | K dexed by S is centred in each letter of S. A function that reads k letters has components only on their subsets, so Proposition C.1 predicts Jacobian energy at degrees 0 and 1 near initialization. Outcome training remains low degree Question. Does the low-degree Jacobian at initialization persist along the outcome-training trajectory? The initialization spectrum, answer-dependent gradient, and AdamW trajectory test successive parts of this mechanism. At initialization. The Jacobian has 99.6% of its energy at degree 0 (Figure 1b), and the answerdependent gradient decays by approximately a factor 0.5 per additional letter: log-linear fits over n = 2–10 give 0.508 for seed 42 (Table 9, R2 = 0.995) and 0.513 and 0.480 for seeds 43 and 44. These gradient measurements use counting modulo 2 over four letters (λ = 0.5) in the experiment architecture with every parameter drawn from N (0, 0.022 ), outside (A2), and a float32 forward pass; gradients are exact over all prompts. The answer-dependent part is only 5×10−3 (n = 2) to 2×10−5 (n = 10) of the full outcome gradient. θ For the spectrum at n = 8, ε = 0.02, a Gaussian parameter direction g satisfies Eg [k( ∂z ∂θ g)r k ] = ∂zθ ∂z k( ∂θ )r k2F . We split ∂θθ g exactly by an orthonormal transform along each letter axis of the 48 grid, 1069 averaging 16 directions over all eight (s0 , w⋆ ) pairs. Per-seed spectra and gradient fits are in the accompanying code repository. n θ ⊤ ) (y − u)]∥ answer-dependent gradient ∥E[( ∂z ∂θ step supervision gradient ratio 8.6e−4 1.8e−2 2.5e−4 3.0e−2 6.5e−5 3.9e−2 2.1e−5 4.0e−2 1,849 7.6e−6 4.2e−2 5,561 3.6e−6 4.3e−2 11,708 Table 9: The answer-dependent gradient decays with horizon while the step gradient does not. Gradient norms at initialization, seed 42. During training. At K = 2, n = 8, ε = 0.02 and seed 42, under the AdamW protocol of Appendix E.1, the share of Jacobian energy above degree 1 stays at most 2.4 × 10−6 through 6k updates. It rises to 0.155 at 8k updates, when the trajectory is escaping (Table 10). outcome updates share above degree 1 accuracy initialization 1,000 3,000 6,000 8,000 (escaping) 2.4e−6 1.1e−8 1.1e−8 2.0e−6 0.155 – 52% 48% 48% 55% Table 10: Outcome training remains low degree until escape. Jacobian energy above degree 1 and held-out answer accuracy. Robustness. Tables 11 and 12 report nonzero-readout, seed, initialization-scale and deeper-model sweeps. The first-occurrence control is learned from answers, whereas modulo-2 counting retains a step/outcome gap. At K = 3, n = 8, outcome accuracy after 32k updates is 100.0, 67.7, 99.9% for seeds 42–44, an explicit moderate-horizon exception. In Table 11, panel (a) reports 8k accuracies for ε = 0.02, seeds 42–44; parentheses count runs reaching 90% by 16k updates for step and 32k for outcome. Panel (b) uses 20 seeds per horizon and 8k updates; n = 4 is excluded because there are fewer distinct prompts than training examples. Panel (c) uses seeds 42–44 and 8k updates. At ε = 0.5 both losses are unstable at this learning rate. The ε6 rule-credit scale at 0.1 is 56 ≈ 1.6 × 104 times that at 0.02, a qualitative comparison outside the certified small-initialization regime. Both exact executors remain correct at every tested horizon. For the first-occurrence row in Table 12, the 12 runs use ε = 0.02, seeds 42–44, and report 8k accuracies; parentheses count runs reaching 90% by 16k updates. The deeper-model sweeps use four or six blocks at n = 8, 12; 10 of 12 counting step runs exceed 80%, while outcome stays near chance. Setting Runs step outcome chance 57–95 (3/3) 100 (3/3) 17–26 (3/3) 74–100 (3/3) 49–55 (0/3) 49–51 (0/3) 49–52 (0/3) 49–52 (0/3) (b) Seed sweep: ε = 0.02, 20 seeds per horizon count, K = 2, n = 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16 120 5, 8, 13, 13, 13, 8 of 20 0 of 20 each (median 49.8–50.8) (c) Initialization-scale sweep: three seeds ε = 0.1, n = 8 / 16 3/3 ε = 0.5, n = 8 / 16 3/3 100 / 100 8–69 / 0–1 63–65 / 48–50 48–53 / 50–52 (a) Horizon sweep: ε = 0.02, three seeds count, K = 2, n = 8 count, K = 2, n = 12 count, K = 2, n = 16 count, K = 2, n = 24 Table 11: Modulo-2 outcome runs remain unsolved across horizons, seeds and initialization scales. Held-out exact-continuation accuracy (%); solved counts are in parentheses. Outcome (a) GPT-2L Step Chance (b) Qwen2.5 LoRA (c) Qwen2.5 on BBH Answer accuracy (%) seed variation Boolean-8 Register Boolean-8 3 objects 5 objects 7 objects Figure 4: Step supervision also helps beyond counting. Held-out answer accuracy for (a) GPT-2L, (b) Qwen2.5-0.5B LoRA, and (c) Qwen2.5-0.5B LoRA on the official 250 BIG-Bench Hard items at each object count. Points show medians, thick intervals the interquartile range, and thin intervals the full seed range; GPT-2L Boolean-8 uses five seeds, other groups three. Dashed segments show chance; exact values and protocols are in Table 13 and Appendix E. Setting Runs step outcome chance first occurrence, n = 8–24 count, K = 2, 4 or 6 blocks, n = 8, 12 first occurrence, 4 or 6 blocks – 80–99 (12/12) 31–100 (10/12 above 80%) 95–100 71–100 (11/12, by 16k) 47.8–50.9 95–100 – – Table 12: The first-occurrence control is learned from answers; deeper counting models retain the supervision gap. Held-out accuracy (%), with the success criteria stated in the prose. E.4 Horizon–contraction sweep Question and setup. Does outcome performance depend on the remaining low-degree signal λn , rather than horizon n alone? For counting modulo 2, we vary alphabet size A ∈ {4, 6, 10, 20}, giving λ = 1 − ∈ {0.5, 0.67, 0.8, 0.9}, A and horizons n ∈ {6, 8, 10, 12, 16}. Each of the 20 cells uses three paired seeds (42–44) and 8k AdamW updates under Appendix E.1. Result. Outcome accuracy has Spearman correlation 0.91 with λn , but only −0.36 with n. After n n subtracting the state-only prior 1+λ 2 , the correlation with λ falls to 0.15 (Figure 3b,c). Cell means are 2 points below to 7 above the prior (median +0.2); when λn ≤ 0.04, every cell mean is within 1.5 points. Thus most learned outcome signal is explained by the accessible low-degree prior. No outcome run reaches 90%; step runs reach it in 17 of 60. The full grid, including per-seed step accuracies, is in the accompanying code repository. E.5 Purpose. Synthetic state-tracking tasks test how degree structure relates to the supervision gap; pretrained models and BIG-Bench Hard test whether the qualitative pattern transfers to a different setting. Beyond counting Synthetic state tracking. Boolean-8 has 4 bits (K = 16) and NOT, CNOT, SWAP and Toffoli gates on random wires (n = 8); the register machine has (x, y) ∈ Z217 , five instructions, n = 16 and answer x. Their answers put 98.9% and 99.9% of their variance at degrees ≥ 2, while first occurrence keeps a degree-1 part at every n (there 1[y = 1] = b1 , so Ek(y − u){1} k2 ≥ p(1 − p)). GPT-2L uses character tokens. 1109 Pretrained models and BIG-Bench Hard. The LoRA runs test transfer of the qualitative mechanism beyond the controlled model. On Boolean-8 (mean±SD over seeds), SmolLM2-135M LoRA reaches 9.9 ± 0.2 (outcome) and 89.7 ± 4.9 (step); eight times the outcome budget gives 11.8 ± 2.2 (SmolLM2) and 10.0 ± 0.9 (Qwen2.5); answer-first formats give 8.0 ± 0.9, 9.3 ± 0.3 and 10.1 ± 1.0 (GPT-2L, SmolLM2, Qwen2.5), so they do not reproduce the step benefit. Benchmark setup and results. Training items come from the benchmark’s templates with new assignments and swaps; testing uses the official 250 items per size, all of which parse and rebuild exactly. Traces list every holder’s item with its option letter (“Alice has Ulysses (A)”); with item names alone, answers stayed at the letter base rate. With m objects a random swap gives λ = mp−1 m−1 (m−1 2 ) with p = m , which is 0, 2 and 3 for m = 3, 5, 7, and the share of answer variance at degrees ≤ 1 (2) is λ2n + nλ2(n−1) (1 − λ2 ): 0, 1.56% and 3.34%. Zero-shot accuracy is 33.6, 16.4 and 11.2%. At three objects, outcome runs select the most common option, whose base rate is 35.2%. Five objects reverse the ordering that the low-degree share predicts: outcome training reaches 80–94% there but 15–24% at seven objects, whose share is larger. One of three five-object step runs fails (46% on real items, 45% on generated ones). Five objects are therefore an exception: outcome training succeeds despite less low-degree variance than seven objects. We treat the degree calculation as a mechanistic guide, not a complete predictor of pretrained-model optimization; these LoRA runs are outside the theorem assumptions. For the BBH spectrum measurement, we split all 63 swap sequences at fixed scene, assignment and query, using the three option logits and the LoRA up-projection Jacobian. For every task in Table 13, λ is the largest singular value of the averaged move permutation on mean-zero state vectors (Definition 2 for counting). It determines the low-degree share for counting and BBH. For Boolean8 and the register machine, it is only the slowest of many state modes: the single-mode estimate λ2n + nλ2(n−1) (1 − λ2 ) gives 20% and 56%, far above the measured 1.1% and 0.08% degree-≤ 1 shares. The summary counting rows use a zero readout and ten paired seeds with 8k updates for modulo 2 (mean accuracy, solved seeds in parentheses), and 32k updates with seeds 42–44 for modulo 3. BBH rows test on the 750 official items, 250 per size. n λ outcome step chance two-block two-block two-block GPT-2L GPT-2L Qwen2.5-0.5B LoRA 8–24 0.50 0.66 – 0.82 0.95 0.82 49.8 (0/10) 30–50 71–100 7.7 6.3 11.1 93.1 (8/10) 80–99 99.9 98.3 – 6.25 5.9 6.25 Qwen2.5-0.5B LoRA Qwen2.5-0.5B LoRA Qwen2.5-0.5B LoRA 0.50 0.67 35.2 80.0–94.4 15.2–24.4 98.4–100 46–100 96.4–100 Task Model count mod 2 count mod 3 first occurrence (control) Boolean-8 register machine Boolean-8 BBH, 3 objects BBH, 5 objects BBH, 7 objects Table 13: Step supervision usually helps when the answer has little low-degree signal. Held-out accuracy (%); ranges are over seeds. Detailed protocols and per-seed values are in the accompanying code repository. E.6 Joint routing runs Table 1 uses the following protocol. Model Task Initialization Trained Optimizer Success two-block executor architecture (Appendix B) counting modulo 2, four letters, p = 41 , MLP biases of magnitude 1–2, fan-in-scaled Gaussian weights, embeddings of variance dres all scaled by ε = 0.3; relative biases and readout zero query/key maps, relative biases, readout (embeddings, values, MLPs fixed) full-batch gradient descent, step size 2, 4,096 training prompts every state prediction on 16,384 held-out prompts has positive margin Of 17 runs, 16 solve; the remaining n = 12 run reaches 99.95% by 300k updates. The outcome baseline uses the same initialization, trained parameter subset, step size and data, with only the 1131 final count supervised; no outcome run solves within 300k updates. Implementation and per-run measurements are available in the accompanying code repository. F Reproducibility Code. Code, configurations, seeds, reproduction commands, numerical proof checks and per-run measurements are available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/trace. The accompanying code repository provides an exact implementation of the executors, a numerical check of the reduced flow of Theorem 4, and instructions for reproducing every experiment. Compute and data. Runs use one NVIDIA A100 80GB GPU: about one minute per 8k counting updates and fifteen minutes per 800-update LoRA run; exploratory runs used more compute than reported. Data are synthetic; the accompanying code repository also contains the official BIG-Bench Hard items, unchanged. Software and assets: PyTorch 2.13 (BSD-3-Clause), Transformers 5.16 and PEFT 0.20 (Apache-2.0), SmolLM2-135M [Allal et al., 2025] and Qwen2.5-0.5B [Qwen Team, 2024] (Apache-2.0), and BIG-Bench Hard [Suzgun et al., 2022] (MIT).  